{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's cross-city modulator (evaporative contrast via vegetation/aridity) is internally inconsistent with observed arid-city UHI: it asserts drier surroundings reduce the urban-rural evaporative contrast and thus weaken the nocturnal gap, but arid cities like Phoenix exhibit large nocturnal UHI. If the single best mechanism must explain the larger gap in some cities, an evaporative-deficit mechanism that predicts weaker UHI for the very cities that show strong UHI fails the problem.","counterexample":["Phoenix, AZ: nocturnal UHI is large despite (and partly because of) arid surroundings, contradicting SRC_001's claim that a drier, less vegetated rural reference yields a weaker nocturnal UHI.","Well-watered tropical cities with dense surrounding vegetation often have small nocturnal UHI, opposite to SRC_001's prediction that vegetated surroundings produce a larger gap."],"premise":"The problem asks for the SINGLE mechanism that best explains why the night-time UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others. SRC_001's stated single cross-city modulator is vegetation/irrigation/aridity (the evaporative contrast), but its own derivation shows aridity cuts the wrong way: it says dry, less vegetated surroundings make the urban-rural evaporative contrast smaller and the UHI weaker, implying humid/vegetated surroundings give a larger gap. Empirically, nocturnal UHI is often larger in arid cities (e.g., Phoenix) precisely because irrigation creates an urban oasis effect and/or dry rural surfaces have very low heat capacity/thermal admittance, so vegetation/aridity as the single modulator does not consistently determine the gap.","target":"fb6feef09e1c1ca6ff62f9c3d038a2e13984eca2bf31f9e37e4c4b343ca353a2"}]}